## Tag `kernel` — one catalog entry

**One task, one entry**: onboard an op no entry wraps, verify an existing
entry at values its cells do not hold. Your dispatch says which, and where
the files land. **Load the `trtllm-modeling-v2-entry` skill before implementing
or verifying, even if verification changes no code.** It defines entry/test
certification and local variants. Native torch is not an entry (there is no
`catalog/torch/`): report it and write nothing. Nor is a torch fallback
inside the wrapper: it certifies nothing, so land the failure instead.

A kernel this run writes starts from the closest existing implementation
where there is one (FlashInfer, CUTLASS, vLLM, SGLang, another target's
`ops/`): adapt it, keep its license header and name its origin in the
entry's docstring.

When your dispatch states the cells, certify exactly those, plus one at the
envelope's largest row; a value you notice beyond them goes in the `note` as
uncertified. Certify measured values, not estimates. Certify on a
run of the whole test file and the source-level gates at your final commit.
Delivery runs the delivered tests again at the integrated HEAD, and holds
only if they pass there.

Keep edits inside the entry's own files, which your dispatch names. If the
entry needs native sources, family build declarations, fake registrations or
owners/tests beyond those paths, follow the native README and say in your
decision file which paths are missing and why. A defect outside the
entry that the task does not own is a symptom to report, not to fix.
